00:12:10,940 --> 00:12:15,040
The position of the pole represents the probability of a quantum particle.

224
00:12:15,840 --> 00:12:22,140
But in quantum mechanics, particles also have a hidden wave-like property that interferes with other particles.

225
00:12:22,300 --> 00:12:23,500
Right, the weird wavy stuff.

226
00:12:23,720 --> 00:12:24,120
Exactly.

227
00:12:24,360 --> 00:12:27,780
So in the flag space, you don't just track the pole, you attach a flag to it.

228
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:32,900
The 2D flag system tracks not just where the pole is, but the exact geometric angle the flag is pointing.

229
00:12:32,920 --> 00:12:33,500
Oh, that makes sense.

230
00:12:33,500 --> 00:12:46,000
So instead of a magical abstract number that mathematically flips signs in an equation somewhere, you have a literal matrix that physically rotates the flag around the pole by 90 degrees.

231
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:51,320
So the complex algebra reappears purely as real geometric matrix operations.

232
00:12:52,020 --> 00:12:54,520
The geometry is finally uncompressed and visible.

233
00:12:54,660 --> 00:12:55,740
We can see the flag turning.

234
00:12:55,900 --> 00:12:56,320
Exactly.

235
00:12:56,640 --> 00:13:00,260
But the sources note that this introduces a massive new tension, right?

236
00:13:00,780 --> 00:13:08,620
When you do this uncompressing, especially when you start looking at composite systems, like multiple quantum particles interacting and entangling with each other,

237
00:13:08,900 --> 00:13:12,660
this expanded real number space requires way more parameters.

238
00:13:12,900 --> 00:13:13,440
It does.

